To convert markdown-formatted text to HTML-formatted text in C++, you would typically need a library that supports markdown parsing and conversion to HTML. However, if you want to implement a basic conversion function yourself, you can start by handling some common markdown elements like headers, bold, italic, and links. Below is a simple example of how you might start implementing such a function. Note that this is a very basic implementation and does not cover all markdown features.

First, you need to include the necessary headers:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <regex>
```

Then, you can define a function that converts markdown to HTML. This function will handle some basic markdown elements:

```cpp
std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html = markdown;

    // Convert headers
    html = std::regex_replace(html, std::regex("^#\\s+(.*)$"), "<h1>$1</h1>"); // Level 1 header
    html = std::regex_replace(html, std::regex("^##\\s+(.*)$"), "<h2>$1</h2>"); // Level 2 header
    html = std::regex_replace(html, std::regex("^###\\s+(.*)$"), "<h3>$1</h3>"); // Level 3 header

    // Convert bold text
    html = std::regex_replace(html, std::regex("\\*\\*(.*?)\\*\\*"), "<strong>$1</strong>");
    html = std::regex_replace(html, std::regex("__([^_]+)__"), "<strong>$1</strong>");

    // Convert italic text
    html = std::regex_replace(html, std::regex("\\*(.*?)\\*"), "<em>$1</em>");
    html = std::regex_replace(html, std::regex("_([^_]+)_"), "<em>$1</em>");

    // Convert links
    html = std::regex_replace(html, std::regex("\\[(.*?)\\]\\((.*?)\\)"), "<a href=\"$2\">$1</a>");

    // Convert newlines to <br>
    html = std::regex_replace(html, std::regex("\n"), "<br>");

    return html;
}
```

This function uses regular expressions to find and replace markdown patterns with their HTML equivalents. Here's a brief explanation of what each part does:

- Headers: It looks for lines starting with `#`, `##`, or `###` followed by a space and some text, and converts them to `<h1>`, `<h2>`, and `<h3>` tags, respectively.
- Bold text: It looks for text enclosed in double asterisks (`**text**`) or underscores (`__text__`) and converts it to `<strong>text</strong>`.
- Italic text: It looks for text enclosed in single asterisks (`*text*`) or underscores (`_text_`) and converts it to `<em>text</em>`.
- Links: It looks for text enclosed in square brackets followed by text in parentheses (`[text](url)`) and converts it to `<a href="url">text</a>`.
- Newlines: It replaces newline characters with `<br>` tags to ensure proper line breaks in HTML.

This is a very basic implementation and does not cover all markdown features or edge cases. For a more comprehensive solution, consider using a dedicated markdown parser library like `pandoc` or `marked`.

